From: Erfan Abdi <erfangplus@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] apklayer: cameraserver trusts the instance's own Linux uid to name a client

Every process of an apklayer instance (the app JVMs, cameraserver itself) runs as the desktop user's Linux uid; an app's Android uid
reaches services as the binder work source of its Java calls (frameworks/native 0005), but its native calls (android.hardware.Camera's
JNI through libcamera_client) carry the kernel uid only, beside an AttributionSource naming the app's uid. A caller with cameraserver's
own uid is one of the instance's processes, so its client uid is taken as given (as for mediaserver on a device); without this every
Camera1 open fails with "Untrusted caller ... trying to forward camera access".
On Ubuntu Touch (uid 32011) libbinder 0007 makes that caller uid AID_SYSTEM, so system is trusted too.
Against frameworks/av 937eb9047ec2 (REVISION). Apply: patches/apply aosp <LineageOS tree>.
---
diff --git a/services/camera/libcameraservice/utils/AttributionAndPermissionUtils.cpp b/services/camera/libcameraservice/utils/AttributionAndPermissionUtils.cpp
--- a/services/camera/libcameraservice/utils/AttributionAndPermissionUtils.cpp
+++ b/services/camera/libcameraservice/utils/AttributionAndPermissionUtils.cpp
@@ -213,6 +213,9 @@ bool AttributionAndPermissionUtils::isTrustedCallingUid(uid_t uid) {
         case AID_RADIO:        // telephony
             return true;
         default:
+            // apklayer patch: the instance's processes share cameraserver's Linux uid (an app's native calls name their uid in the attribution);
+            // libbinder 0007 reports that uid to a service as AID_SYSTEM (a desktop user in Android's app range, Ubuntu Touch's phablet)
+            if (uid == getuid() || uid == AID_SYSTEM) return true;
             return false;
     }
 }
